  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low will develop off the Florida \ncoast late this week and will pass through the local area Sunday\ninto Monday.\n\nHigh pressure over the Mid-Atlantic area will move offshore by \nSaturday. Meanwhile, surface low pressure will develop and \norganize east of the Florida coast late this week and will track\nalong the coast this weekend. Conditions remain dry over the \nlocal area through Saturday, and then there is uncertainty as to\nthe exact track and strength of that low.\n\nThe 12Z/18 operational GFS is closer to the coast and weaker \ncompared to the 12Z/18 AIGFS, which has much more rainfall over \neastern Delaware and New Jersey. The 12Z/18 operational ECMWF \nactually has a secondary low spin off ahead of the primary low, \nand then the primary low passes well east of the New Jersey \ncoast on Monday and Monday night. The 12Z/18 EC-AIFS is similar \nto the AIGFS in that it is just off the New Jersey coast and has\nwidespread heavy rain a bit farther west, across New Jersey and\njust west of the I-95 corridor. There is still plenty of time \nfor the detail to sort themselves out, but the highest PoPs look\nto be at least Sunday and Sunday night. PoPs on Sunday are up \nto 60 percent for most of Delmarva and most of New Jersey and 40\nto 50 percent for southeast Pennsylvania and northern New \nJersey. The national Hurricane Center has a 40 percent chance \nfor cyclone formation in the next 7 days over the western \nAtlantic Ocean.\n\nA strong cold front follows through Monday night and cold air \nreturns for the new week.",
